Fix TDM warp distribution over-subscribing dimensions smaller than num_warps.

tdmGetWarpDistribution() first hands out warps per dimension with the bound
warpsOut[i] * 2 <= blockShape[i], then unconditionally folds any leftover
warps into the last dimension. When the block is smaller than num_warps the
fallback pushes warpsOut[numDims - 1] past blockShape[numDims - 1], so
getTDMLinearLayout() computes messageShape[i] = blockShape[i] / warpsPerCTA[i]
== 0. identityStandardND() then calls strided1D(0, ...), which returns
LinearLayout::empty() and drops the "message" input dimension. The subsequent
applyLinearLayout(..., {{kMessage, 0}, ...}) in fillTDMDescriptor() fails with

  Dimensions must match, ignoring order, but they don't.
  Got dims: ["message", "warp", "block"] and ["warp", "block"]

Reproduced on gfx1250 (the only target where has_tdm_support() is true) with a
1D reduce tile of 4 elements and num_warps = 8: the bounded loop stops at
warpsOut[0] = 4 with remainingWarps = 2, and the fallback raises it to 8.

Surplus warps do not need a dimension to live on. getTDMLinearLayout() already
asserts only totalWarps >= prod(warpsPerCTA), assigns zero basis rows to the
unused warpId bits, and relies on getFreeVariableMasks() so the lowering
predicates those warps off. Dropping the fallback lets that path handle the
case it was written for. distributeTDMWarpsAlignToPartition() in
TDMUtility.cpp already ends with a bounded loop and no fallback, so this also
makes the two distribution helpers consistent.

tdmGetAdjustedBlockShape() is the other caller and is unaffected: for numDims
== 1 it divides by numWarps and never reads warps[], and for numDims >= 2 the
fallback can only fire once every warpsOut[i] has reached blockShape[i], where
divideCeil(blockShape[i], warpsOut[i]) is 1 both before and after this change
(TDM block shapes are powers of two). The host-side driver.c copy of the
header sees the same values. Verified by sweeping both versions over all
power-of-two extents in 1D/2D/3D for num_warps 1..64: tdmGetAdjustedBlockShape
is identical in every case, and the cases where warpsPerCTA changes are exactly
the cases where the old code produced warpsOut[i] > blockShape[i].

A companion assert guarding this invariant at its use site lives in
tdm_message_shape_assert.patch.

Lifecycle: Upstream this fix to triton-lang/triton and remove the patch in the next integration.

--- a/third_party/amd/backend/include/TDMCommon.h
+++ b/third_party/amd/backend/include/TDMCommon.h
@@ -18,22 +18,28 @@
 // Compute warp distribution across dimensions.
 // Distributes warps starting from the first dimension, assigning as many
 // warps as possible without exceeding the block shape.
+//
+// The product of the returned counts can be smaller than numWarps when the
+// block is too small to keep every warp busy.  The surplus warps are left
+// undistributed on purpose: getTDMLinearLayout() gives their warpId bits
+// zero basis rows, getFreeVariableMasks() reports them as free variables,
+// and the lowering predicates those warps off.  Folding the leftovers into
+// the last dimension instead makes warpsOut[i] exceed blockShape[i], the
+// message shape blockShape[i] / warpsOut[i] truncates to zero, and the
+// "message" input dimension vanishes from the TDM layout entirely.
 static inline void tdmGetWarpDistribution(const int64_t *blockShape,
                                           int numDims, int numWarps,
                                           int *warpsOut) {
   for (int i = 0; i < numDims; ++i)
     warpsOut[i] = 1;
 
   int remainingWarps = numWarps;
   for (int i = 0; i < numDims && remainingWarps > 1; ++i) {
     while (remainingWarps > 1 && warpsOut[i] * 2 <= blockShape[i]) {
       warpsOut[i] *= 2;
       remainingWarps /= 2;
     }
   }
-
-  if (remainingWarps > 1)
-    warpsOut[numDims - 1] *= remainingWarps;
 }
 
 // Compute per-warp block sizes after distributing warps.
